= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The statement requires $K\ne\varnothing$; an empty set is closed and convex but contains no minimizer. Assume this necessary condition and put $d=\inf_{g\in K}\|g-f\|_p$. Since $K$ is closed and $f\notin K$, $d>0$. Choose a <minimizing sequence> $g_j\in K$ with $\|g_j-f\|_p\to d$.

The midpoint $(g_j+g_k)/2$ lies in the <convex set> $K$. Applying the stated <Clarkson inequality> to $g_j-f$ and $g_k-f$ gives
$$
\|g_j-g_k\|_p^p
\leq2^{p-1}\bigl(\|g_j-f\|_p^p+\|g_k-f\|_p^p\bigr)
-\|g_j+g_k-2f\|_p^p
\leq2^{p-1}\bigl(\|g_j-f\|_p^p+\|g_k-f\|_p^p\bigr)-2^pd^p.
$$
The right side tends to zero, so $g_j$ is a <Cauchy sequence>. By <completeness of Lp spaces>, proved in Question 2(a), it converges to some $h$. Closedness puts $h$ in $K$, and continuity of the norm gives $\boxed{\|h-f\|_p=d}$. This is the <closest point theorem for a closed convex subset of Lp>. The same inequality applied to two minimizers forces their difference to be zero, so the minimizer is also unique.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $g\in K$, <convexity> gives $h+t(g-h)\in K$ for $0\leq t\leq1$. Thus $F(t)=\|h-f+t(g-h)\|_p^p$ has a right minimum at $t=0$ and $F'(0)\geq0$. The <derivative of a power of the Lp norm> is
$$
F'(0)=p\,\operatorname{Re}\int_{\mathbb R^n}|h-f|^{p-2}\overline{(h-f)}(g-h)\,dx.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}\int_{\mathbb R^n}|h-f|^{p-2}\overline{(h-f)}(h-g)\,dx\leq0.}
$$
The printed hint has coefficient $2$ where the general coefficient is $p$. This does not affect the desired sign, since $p>0$. The complex conjugation is important for complex-valued functions; for real functions it can be omitted.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the printed bilinear pairing $\phi_u(v)=\int uv$, which is complex linear in both arguments. For the <conjugate exponents> $p,q$, <Holder inequality> gives $|\phi_u(v)|\leq\|u\|_q\|v\|_p$, proving well-definedness, continuity, and $\|\phi_u\|\leq\|u\|_q$. Linearity of $u\mapsto\phi_u$ follows from linearity of integration.

For $u\ne0$, take
$$
v=\frac{\overline u\,|u|^{q-2}}{\|u\|_q^{q-1}},
$$
with the numerator defined as zero where $u=0$. Since $(q-1)p=q$, this has $\|v\|_p=1$ and $\phi_u(v)=\|u\|_q$. Hence $\boxed{\|\phi_u\|=\|u\|_q}$; the same identity holds for $u=0$. This also proves injectivity.

For surjectivity, let $\Lambda$ be a nonzero bounded <linear functional> on $L^p$. Choose $f$ with $\Lambda(f)=1$ and let $K=\ker\Lambda$. The <closest point theorem for a closed convex subset of Lp> supplies the nearest point $h\in K$; write $w=f-h\ne0$. For every $z\in K$, the whole line $h+tz$ lies in $K$, so the norm derivative at the minimum vanishes for real $t$. Applying this also to $iz$ shows that the complex linear functional
$$
\Psi(v)=\int |w|^{p-2}\overline w\,v
$$
vanishes on $K$. Since $\Lambda(w)=1$, each $v-\Lambda(v)w$ is in $K$, giving $\Psi(v)=\Lambda(v)\Psi(w)$ and $\Psi(w)=\|w\|_p^p$. Therefore
$$
\Lambda(v)=\int u\,v,\qquad
u=\frac{|w|^{p-2}\overline w}{\|w\|_p^p}\in L^q.
$$
Membership follows from $(p-1)q=p$. The zero functional is represented by $u=0$. This proves the asserted <Lp duality> isometric isomorphism, including surjectivity, without a separate representation theorem.

